# The ledger of `compress`: what Go's `compress/flate`, `compress/zlib` and
# `compress/gzip` (Go 1.27, with `compress/bzip2` and `compress/lzw` beside
# them) and Python's `zlib` and `gzip` (3.15; `compression.zlib` and
# `compression.gzip` are the same modules again) let a program do, one block
# per capability, and what Rebel answers. Rust's standard library has no
# compression, so its line is `-` in every block. The lists are this
# machine's, 2026-11-16: `go doc -all` on each package, `dir()` of each
# Python module and of its objects, and `inspect.signature`. Every stream in
# the package's tests was made by Python or by hand and given to Python
# first; the cross-check in the scratch folder read 152 of Python's streams
# and had Python read 312 of Rebel's, all alike.

capability compress/at-once  bytes compressed whole into one stream
  heroes  compress.compress
  go      flate.NewWriter, zlib.NewWriter, gzip.NewWriter, Writer.Write, Writer.Close (into a bytes.Buffer)
  rust    -
  python  zlib.compress, gzip.compress
  note    Go has no call that compresses a `[]byte` whole; a writer over a buffer does it. Python's `gzip.compress` writes an mtime of 0 by default since 3.15, as `defaults` does, so the same bytes give the same stream

capability compress/decompress-at-once  a stream decompressed whole, under a limit
  heroes  compress.decompress
  go      flate.NewReader, zlib.NewReader, gzip.NewReader, Reader.Read, io.ReadAll
  rust    -
  python  zlib.decompress, gzip.decompress
  note    a `limit` is required, where neither Go nor Python asks for one: a few bytes expand to gigabytes. Bytes after a raw or zlib stream fail `invalid`, where Python's `zlib.decompress` ignores them; `decompress_leading` is the call that allows them. After a gzip member, zero bytes are padding and another member may follow, as Python's `gzip.decompress` reads them; an empty input fails `invalid` as Go's `gzip.NewReader` fails, where Python answers no bytes

capability compress/leading  a stream at the start of other bytes, and where it ended
  heroes  compress.decompress_leading, compress.unused
  go      gzip.Reader.Multistream
  rust    -
  python  zlib.decompressobj, Decompress.unused_data, Decompress.eof
  note    what zip needs where a member's sizes come after its data. A gzip member is read alone here, as Go's `Multistream(false)` and Python's `decompressobj(wbits=31)` read it

capability compress/formats  what wraps the data: raw DEFLATE, zlib, gzip
  heroes  compress.Format
  go      compress/flate, compress/zlib, compress/gzip
  rust    -
  python  zlib.compress(wbits=), zlib.decompress(wbits=), zlib.MAX_WBITS, zlib.DEFLATED
  note    Python chooses the wrapping and the window by the sign and the size of one number, `wbits`; here they are a variant and a field

capability compress/detect  zlib told from gzip by the first bytes
  heroes  compress.format_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.decompress(wbits=47), zlib.decompressobj(wbits=47)
  note    Python decompresses whichever it finds; here the format is asked, then named. Raw DEFLATE has no header and is never told

capability compress/levels  how hard the compressor works
  heroes  compress.Options, compress.NO_COMPRESSION, compress.BEST_SPEED, compress.DEFAULT_LEVEL, compress.BEST_COMPRESSION
  go      flate.NoCompression, flate.BestSpeed, flate.BestCompression, flate.DefaultCompression, zlib.NoCompression, zlib.BestSpeed, zlib.BestCompression, zlib.DefaultCompression, gzip.NoCompression, gzip.BestSpeed, gzip.BestCompression, gzip.DefaultCompression, zlib.NewWriterLevel, gzip.NewWriterLevel
  rust    -
  python  zlib.Z_NO_COMPRESSION, zlib.Z_BEST_SPEED, zlib.Z_BEST_COMPRESSION, zlib.Z_DEFAULT_COMPRESSION, compress(level=), gzip.compress(compresslevel=), GzipFile(compresslevel=), gzip.open(compresslevel=)
  note    0 to 9 as in both; the default is 6 by number, where Go and Python say -1. A level out of 0 to 9 fails `invalid`, as Go's `NewWriterLevel` errs and Python raises. What each level tries is Rebel's own: 4 to 4,096 earlier places per byte, lazy matching from level 4

capability compress/strategy  how repeats are looked for, beside the level
  heroes  compress.Strategy
  go      flate.HuffmanOnly, zlib.HuffmanOnly, gzip.HuffmanOnly
  rust    -
  python  zlib.Z_DEFAULT_STRATEGY, zlib.Z_FILTERED, zlib.Z_HUFFMAN_ONLY, zlib.Z_RLE, zlib.Z_FIXED, compressobj(strategy=)
  note    Go has only Huffman-only, as a level; Python's five are here. `filtered` writes repeats shorter than 6 bytes as bytes, which is how zlib's manual describes its effect; `fixed` may still write a stored block where that is smaller

capability compress/window  the farthest a repeat reaches back
  heroes  compress.Options, compress.MAX_WINDOW_BITS, compress.MIN_WINDOW_BITS
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compressobj(wbits=), zlib.MAX_WBITS
  note    9 to 15, as Python takes them; zlib's header says it. A reader keeps 32,768 bytes whatever the header says

capability compress/dictionary  a preset dictionary written with and read with
  heroes  compress.Options, compress.with_dictionary
  go      flate.NewWriterDict, flate.NewReaderDict, zlib.NewWriterLevelDict, zlib.NewReaderDict, zlib.ErrDictionary
  rust    -
  python  compressobj(zdict=), decompressobj(zdict=)
  note    raw and zlib only, as in both; gzip refuses one. A zlib stream that needs one names its Adler-32, and a missing or different dictionary fails `invalid`; a dictionary given to a zlib stream that names none is not used, as Go does

capability compress/in-memory  a stream compressed piece by piece without a stream to write to
  heroes  compress.Compressor, compress.compressor, compress.compress_part, compress.compress_end, compress.is_ended
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.compressobj, Compress.compress, Compress.flush, zlib.Z_NO_FLUSH, zlib.Z_FINISH
  note    Go writes into a buffer through its writer; Heroes has no stream held in memory, so this is the engine the `Writer` uses, and a program may hold it alone. Fed after its end it aborts, which `is_ended` asks first

capability compress/in-memory-read  a stream decompressed piece by piece, at most so many bytes a call
  heroes  compress.Decompressor, compress.decompressor, compress.decompress_part, compress.is_done
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.decompressobj, Decompress.decompress, Decompress.decompress(max_length=), Decompress.flush, Decompress.unconsumed_tail, Decompress.eof
  note    the input not yet read is held inside, so there is no unconsumed tail to hand back; `is_done` is Python's `eof`, true only once every byte is handed out

capability compress/flush  what was written made readable before the end
  heroes  compress.compress_flush, compress.flush, compress.Flush
  go      flate.Writer.Flush, zlib.Writer.Flush, gzip.Writer.Flush
  rust    -
  python  Compress.flush, zlib.Z_SYNC_FLUSH, zlib.Z_FULL_FLUSH, GzipFile.flush
  note    `sync` is Go's Flush and Python's Z_SYNC_FLUSH; `full` is Z_FULL_FLUSH, which Go does not have

capability compress/partial-flush  flushes that end a block without aligning, or stop between blocks
  heroes  refused: they serve zlib's own callers (PNG writers, inflateBack); `sync` makes everything readable, at most five bytes dearer
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  zlib.Z_PARTIAL_FLUSH, zlib.Z_BLOCK, zlib.Z_TREES
  note    -

capability compress/copy-state  a compressor or decompressor copied, each going on apart
  heroes  built-in =
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Compress.copy, Decompress.copy
  note    a `Compressor` and a `Decompressor` hold no handle, so `=` copies them

capability compress/write-stream  compressed bytes written to a stream as they come
  heroes  compress.Writer, compress.writer, compress.write, compress.finish
  go      flate.NewWriter, flate.Writer.Write, flate.Writer.Close, zlib.NewWriter, zlib.Writer.Write, zlib.Writer.Close, gzip.NewWriter, gzip.Writer.Write, gzip.Writer.Close, gzip.Writer.Header
  rust    -
  python  gzip.GzipFile(mode="wb", fileobj=), GzipFile.write, GzipFile.writelines, GzipFile.close
  note    `finish` writes the end and leaves the stream open, for its owner to close (Go's `Close` does the same to its `io.Writer`); a write after it fails `closed`

capability compress/read-stream  decompressed bytes read from a stream as they are needed
  heroes  compress.Reader, compress.reader, compress.read, compress.read_rest
  go      flate.NewReader, zlib.NewReader, gzip.NewReader, gzip.Reader.Read, gzip.Reader.Close, flate.Reader, flate.ReadError
  rust    -
  python  gzip.GzipFile(mode="rb", fileobj=), GzipFile.read, GzipFile.read1, GzipFile.readinto, GzipFile.readinto1, GzipFile.peek
  note    the reader reads its stream 65,536 bytes at a time and only when it needs a byte, so it never waits for bytes after the end of a stream; every gzip member is read, as Go's and Python's readers read them

capability compress/copy-streams  one stream compressed or decompressed into another
  heroes  compress.compress_stream, compress.decompress_stream
  go      io.Copy through a Writer or a Reader
  rust    -
  python  shutil.copyfileobj through a GzipFile
  note    each answers how many bytes it wrote

capability compress/limit  a bound on what an input may expand to
  heroes  compress.decompress, compress.decompressor, compress.reader, compress.LIMIT
  go      io.LimitReader (around a Reader)
  rust    -
  python  Decompress.decompress(max_length=)
  note    every function that decompresses takes one and fails `limit` before it would write past it; Python's `max_length` bounds one call and not the stream

capability compress/corrupt  an input that is not what it says, told with where
  heroes  compress.INVALID
  go      flate.CorruptInputError, flate.InternalError, zlib.ErrChecksum, zlib.ErrHeader, gzip.ErrChecksum, gzip.ErrHeader
  rust    -
  python  zlib.error, gzip.BadGzipFile
  note    one code, the message naming the byte, as Go's CorruptInputError gives an offset. Each refusal Python's zlib makes on the fifteen streams made by hand for the tests is made here too: block type 3, a stored length and its complement, symbols 286, 287, 30 and 31, a repeat before the start, a code length code or a literal code over-subscribed or incomplete, a repeat before the first length, no code for the end of a block, more than 286 codes

capability compress/gzip-header  what a gzip member says of its file, written and read
  heroes  compress.Header, compress.header_of, compress.read_header, compress.Options
  go      gzip.Header, gzip.Header.Name, gzip.Header.Comment, gzip.Header.Extra, gzip.Header.ModTime, gzip.Header.OS, gzip.Reader.Header, gzip.Writer.Header
  rust    -
  python  GzipFile(filename=), GzipFile(mtime=), GzipFile.mtime, gzip.compress(mtime=)
  note    the name and the comment are Latin-1 as RFC 1952 says, so a character past U+00FF fails `invalid` when written, as Go errs; when read, a name or comment past 65,535 bytes fails `invalid` (Go refuses 512 bytes or more, Python none; measured). The header's own CRC is checked where it has one, as Go checks it and Python skips it. The time is seconds since 1970 as the format holds it, never a `time.Instant`

capability compress/gzip-members  several gzip members read as one
  heroes  compress.decompress, compress.reader, compress.decompress_leading
  go      gzip.Reader.Multistream
  rust    -
  python  gzip.decompress, GzipFile.read
  note    `decompress` and a `Reader` read every member; `decompress_leading` and a `Decompressor` read one

capability compress/gzip-file  a gzip file opened by its path
  heroes  refused: a path is `fs`'s, a package of the same layer; `fs.open` answers the stream `reader` and `writer` take
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  gzip.open, GzipFile(filename=, mode="ab"), GzipFile(mode="xb"), gzip.open(encoding=, errors=, newline=), GzipFile.name, GzipFile.myfileobj, gzip.READ, gzip.WRITE
  note    appending a member is a `Writer` over a stream opened to append

capability compress/lines  a compressed text read line by line
  heroes  refused: lines are `io`'s, over an `io.Stream`, and a `Reader` is not one (Heroes has no interfaces, README A6); a text read whole is cut by `bytes.lines`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  GzipFile.readline, GzipFile.readlines, GzipFile.__iter__
  note    -

capability compress/seek  a position in the decompressed bytes
  heroes  refused: seeking in a compressed stream is decompressing it again from the start, which a program does by making a new `Reader`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  GzipFile.seek, GzipFile.tell, GzipFile.rewind, GzipFile.seekable
  note    -

capability compress/other-formats  bzip2, LZMA and xz, Zstandard, Brotli
  heroes  compress/zstd.compress, compress/zstd.decompress, compress/brotli.compress, compress/brotli.decompress, compress/bzip2.compress, compress/bzip2.decompress, compress/xz.compress, compress/xz.decompress, compress/xz.compress_lzma, compress/xz.decompress_lzma
  go      compress/bzip2, bzip2.NewReader, bzip2.StructuralError
  rust    -
  python  bz2, lzma, compression.bz2, compression.lzma, compression.zstd
  note    four subpackages, so only the programs that use one carry it (README A2); each has its own ledger, `compress/zstd.ledger`, `compress/brotli.ledger`, `compress/bzip2.ledger` and `compress/xz.ledger`, which name the entries of Go's `compress/bzip2` and of Python's `bz2`, `lzma` and `compression.zstd` block by block. Until 2026-11-18 this block was refused, "not DEFLATE"

capability compress/lzw  LZW, compressed and decompressed
  heroes  refused: LZW is a family whose variants each format fixes (GIF's codes lowest bit first, PDF's and TIFF's highest first, with an early change, as Go's `lzw.LSB` and `lzw.MSB` name two of them), and no format a Rebel package reads needs it apart from the package that reads the format, which holds its own variant; README A1 lists no `compress/lzw`
  go      compress/lzw, lzw.NewReader, lzw.NewWriter, lzw.Order, lzw.LSB, lzw.MSB, lzw.Reader, lzw.Writer
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Unix `compress`'s `.Z` files are LZW too, and no package here reads them

dropped
  go      flate.Writer.Reset, zlib.Writer.Reset, gzip.Writer.Reset, gzip.Reader.Reset, flate.Resetter, zlib.Resetter: reusing a writer's or reader's memory for another stream; here a new one is made
  go      flate.WriteError, flate.ReadError.Offset, flate.WriteError.Offset: errors of the stream underneath, which are `io`'s codes here
  python  zlib.crc32, zlib.adler32, zlib.crc32_combine, zlib.adler32_combine: checksums, `hash.crc32`, `hash.adler32`, `hash.crc32_combine`, `hash.adler32_combine`
  python  zlib.compressobj(memLevel=), zlib.DEF_MEM_LEVEL, zlib.DEF_BUF_SIZE, zlib.decompress(bufsize=), compressobj(method=): memory and buffer sizes of zlib's C library, and a method that has one value
  python  zlib.ZLIB_VERSION, zlib.ZLIB_RUNTIME_VERSION, zlib._ZlibDecompressor: which C library Python links; Rebel stands on none
  python  GzipFile.fileno, GzipFile.isatty, GzipFile.readable, GzipFile.writable, GzipFile.closed, GzipFile.detach, GzipFile.truncate: what every Python file object has
